escent  on these input images to make sure that  every single one of these images is  classified as being Keanu Reeves and  very interestingly it turns out that  these adversarial attacks are not just  in computer vision you can actually  apply the same technique in something  like natural language processing as well  let's have a look so if you think about  websites like Amazon or booking.com  TripAdvisor or things like that a lot of  their information and business value  actually comes from users that post  reviews on the websites and nowadays  most of that information is crunched and  aggregated using things like sentiment  analysis so here you can see a bunch of  very intriguing examples where by only  changing a couple of characters in a  sentence we are able to completely flip  the classification of a natural language  model alright so now that we know how  simple adversarial attacks work what can  we do to defend against them so I'm sure  that many of you will have figured out  that in order to generate an adversarial  example you need access to the gradient  in your network and so if you look at  some of the main computer vision api's  like Google or Amazon or Microsoft that  we have today most of them can be run in  two modes the first mode is where you  simply run an image through the API and  it spits back a bunch of labels that  might be present in that input image and  for every single label you get a  probability and this means that if you  make a slight adjustment to your input  image you can actually see the result on  the probabilities of those labels and  you can figure out if you are headed  towards an adversarial example and so an  obvious defense would be to hide that  probability vector and simply return the  most likely class label  and by doing this an attacker wouldn't  have access to the gradient of our model  but does this really solve our problem  well it turns out unfortunately it  doesn't basically we have two types of  adversarial attacks we have the white  box attacks where you have access to  that gradient to the model and we have  the black box attack where you don't  have that access and so the general idea  behind a black box attack is that you're  gonna use the API itself to label your  own training data so you select any kind  of input image that you want to test you  run it through the API and then that  label is what you get back as a  supervision signal and what you can do  that is you can generate a whole bunch  of images they can be real or they can  be synthetic and you use the labels that  are spit back from the API as training  data and you train your own model to  sort of simulate what the actual API is  doing internally and remember that  characteristic about cross model  generalization well this is why  adversarial attacks are so difficult to  defend against it is because if you have  your own local model and you use it to  generate an adversarial example there is  a very high chance that that adversarial  example will also fool your target  Network and it turns out you can do even  simple stuff than that you can start  from any chosen image add a whole bunch  of noise vectors to it and eventually  you will hit some kind of classification  border where the maximum likelihood  label will switch and then you know that  you've hit the border and then the only  thing you have to do is figure out the  smallest amount of noise that you need  to add to the input image in order to  make it flip so hiding the gradient of a  model doesn't work then there were a  couple of people who suggested that okay  these adversarial examples there might  be a theoretical problem but there won't  be problems in the real world because if  you have something like a self-driving  car that is looking at images then those  images are constantly changing in  distance and angle and direction things  like that so there are a whole bunch of  transformations that are being done to  the images and an adversarial